If you are building a Linux application in Rust that needs to manage or inspect the Internet connectivity status via NetworkManager sockets / D-Bus, you should use the official async Rust crate nmrs (formerly known as networkmanager-rs). It provides strongly typed bindings to control NetworkManager over the system D-Bus socket without executing shell commands.
Here is how you can use it to check for an active internet connection, detect changes, or create a connection:

1. Add Dependencies

Add nmrs and tokio (for the async runtime) to your Cargo.toml:
toml
[dependencies]
nmrs = "0.1" # or latest version
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
Use code with caution.

2. Checking Internet Connectivity & State

NetworkManager maintains a global connectivity state. You can fetch this state directly to see if the device has full internet access:
rust
use nmrs::NetworkManager;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> nmrs::Result<()> {
    // Connects to NetworkManager via the system D-Bus socket
    let nm = NetworkManager::new().await?;

    // Check overall internet connectivity status
    if let Some(connectivity) = nm.connectivity().await {
        println!("Connectivity status: {:?}", connectivity);
        // Possible values include: Full, Limited, Portal, None
    }

    // Check if the system is globally "connected"
    if let Some(state) = nm.state().await {
        println!("NetworkManager State: {:?}", state);
    }

    Ok(())
)
Use code with caution.

3. Real-Time Connectivity Monitoring

Instead of polling the socket, you can subscribe to real-time events via D-Bus signals to know the exact moment the internet goes up or down:
rust
use nmrs::NetworkManager;
use std::time::Duration;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> nmrs::Result<()> {
    let nm = NetworkManager::new().await?;

    // Listen for global connectivity or device changes
    let net_handle = nm.monitor_network_changes(|| {
        println!("Network state or connectivity changed!");
    }).await?;

    // Keep the application running to listen for events
    tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(60)).await;

    // Gracefully stop monitoring
    net_handle.stop().await?;
    Ok(())
}
Use code with caution.

Alternative: Low-level dbus Crate

If you prefer not to use nmrs and want to query the underlying D-Bus socket path (org.freedesktop.NetworkManager) directly, you can use the synchronous networkmanager crate:
rust
use dbus::blocking::Connection;
use networkmanager::NetworkManager;

fn main() -> Result<(), networkmanager::Error> {
    let dbus_connection = Connection::new_system()?;
    let nm = NetworkManager::new(&dbus_connection);

    // Enumerate network interfaces directly
    for dev in nm.get_devices()? {
        println!("Found device: {:?}", dev);
    }
    Ok(())
}
Use code with caution.

